    "summary": "Blues‑heavy early Dead with stellar Cream Puff War and Alice D.",
    "blurb": "The Matrix set delivers a raw, high‑energy blues marathon highlighted by a scorching Cream Puff War solo and the band's original Alice D. Millionaire. Pigpen’s organ and harp drive the groove while Jerry, Phil and Bill lock in tight throughout. Collectors will love the rarity of tracks like One Kind Favor and Look on Yonder Wall.",
    "review": "On December 1, 1966 at The Matrix, the Grateful Dead turned the club into a blues‑filled furnace. From the opening New Minglewood Blues through a rolling three‑set marathon they kept the momentum relentless, trading fiery leads and gritty organ riffs. Jerry Garcia’s soaring solo on “Cream Puff War” is repeatedly praised as stellar, and his lead throughout feels both pop‑bright and raucous. Pigpen’s Vox organ and harp dominate the low end, especially on “Death Don't Have No Mercy,” “Deep Elem Blues,” and the mysterious “Look on Yonder Wall*,” where a guest harmonica (rumored to be Paul Butterfield) adds extra bite. Phil Lesh underpins the set with a solid, driving bass line, while Bill Kreutzmann’s drumming stays tight and propulsive despite the raw recording. Standout moments include the original “Alice D. Millionaire,” the high‑octane “One Kind Favor,” and the double‑dose of “Me And My Uncle.” Though some tape hiss and reduced high frequencies persist, the cleaned‑up soundboard captures the band in good form, making this a must‑listen for early Dead enthusiasts.",
